Prometheus continuously scrapes metrics from targets at fixed intervals, 
stores them as time-series data, and then evaluates alerting rules on those stored metrics.
If a rule condition stays beyond the defined threshold for the configured duration, 
Prometheus fires an alert and sends it to Alertmanager, which then routes notifications to channels like Slack or PagerDuty.
_____________________________________________________________________________________________

Recording rules are used to precompute and store frequently used or expensive PromQL expressions as new time-series metrics.
They improve Prometheus performance, make dashboards faster, and provide reusable, standardized metrics that can be used by both Grafana and alerting rules.

Alerting rules are used to evaluate conditions on metrics and trigger alerts when thresholds or SLO conditions are violated. They do not store data; 

instead, they send alerts to Alertmanager for notification, routing, and deduplication.

In production, alerting rules should be built on top of recording rules, not on raw metrics. This keeps alerts fast, clean, and consistent, and 
reduces load on Prometheus.








